WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.200
Sooner or later someone will wave you down and ask for a tow.

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:07.075
You’re allowed to help, but do it properly.

3
00:00:07.325 --> 00:00:09.150
Look at the diagram.

4
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:15.300
Make the towline fast to strong points, with a bridle that spreads the load between two cleats.

5
00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:18.400
Not one small cleat, and never a rail.

6
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.200
Use a long towline with a sag in it.

7
00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.700
The sag works like a spring and takes up the shock.

8
00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.925
Keep the speed low, well below planing.

9
00:00:28.175 --> 00:00:30.450
Keep everyone clear of the line.

10
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.200
Remember snap-back from lesson eight?

11
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.700
And keep a knife where you can reach it, to cut free in an emergency.

12
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.575
Now the judgment call.

13
00:00:40.825 --> 00:00:45.050
In serious conditions, is towing always the right thing to do?

14
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.675
No.

15
00:00:46.925 --> 00:00:54.475
Often the wiser move is to stand by, keep them company, and call a professional towing service or the Coast Guard.
